    "created_at": "2016-09-15T15:13:58Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-09-15T15:13:58Z",
    "body": "Testcase:\n\n```\n<script>\nvar c = document.createElement(\"canvas\").getContext(\"2d\");\nc.moveTo(0, { valueOf: function() { alert(\"called for finite double\"); } });\nc.moveTo(NaN, { valueOf: function() { alert(\"called for NaN\"); } });\n</script>\n```\n\nResults:\n- Chrome dev: shows both alerts\n- Firefox nightly: shows only the first alert\n- Safari 9: shows both alerts\n- WebKit nightly: shows both alerts\n- Edge 14: shows both alerts\n\nThat shows that everyone except Firefox treats this as an algorithm-steps-level requirement.  Firefox treats it the way @junov describes: just like restricted args that immediately return instead of throwing.  In particular, Firefox has a WebIDL extension it uses for this case, so this is done as part of normal argument processing (aka the \"monkeypatch\" approach I mention above).\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
