    "created_at": "2016-09-15T16:00:46Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-09-15T16:00:46Z",
    "body": "Fwiw, I agree that aligning on either behavior would likely be web-compatible, and would be fine with adding something to IDL if others want to move in that direction too. // cc @cdumez\n\nWhat Gecko implements is an annotation on the whole method that says \"any restricted float/double arg that's not finite means immediate return\".  This only applies to arguments whose type is float/double or nullable versions of those, or a sequence of any of those (e.g. `sequence<float?>`). The sequence thing is needed for `setLineDash`.  I suspect we also make it work for `sequence<sequence<float>>` by accident, now that I think of it....\n\nIn any case, we don't have anything along these lines for unions or dictionary members or anything like that.  If we do add something like this to IDL, I would prefer it be very scoped down so it only needs to be supported in the cases where the legacy canvas stuff needs it (e.g. it should not be a new type usable anywhere).\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
